WEBVTT


00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:05.010
We are planning to knit a
cute little drop shoulder tee.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.890
Now this could be a child's Pinafore
or something else, but the front is

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:15.630
knit in one piece and we want the yoke
section in a different stitch pattern.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.330
Now we know that two stitch patterns
are going to have very different gauge.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.630
Let's take a look at how
to best approach this

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.880
so our Tee ends up the right size.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:29.895
I'm not going to bore you with the math.

00:00:30.104 --> 00:00:34.245
Trust me, and just follow along
and follow the logic here.

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:38.445
We'll knit the lower half of the
sweater in our first stitch pattern.

00:00:39.015 --> 00:00:44.144
Let's say stockinette at 20 stitches
to four inches or 10 centimeters

00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:46.545
or seven stitches to the inch.

00:00:47.144 --> 00:00:49.815
Let's ignore the
difference in rows for now.

00:00:51.165 --> 00:00:54.355
According to the pattern, when
we get to the yoke section the

00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:56.775
knitting will measure 16 inches.

00:00:57.775 --> 00:01:02.035
At this gauge, this
translates to 112 stitches.

00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:08.845
We've swatched and we've established
that our lace gauge is 24 stitches

00:01:08.845 --> 00:01:13.135
to four inches, so instead of
seven stitches to the inch, we're

00:01:13.135 --> 00:01:15.295
looking at six stitches to the inch.

00:01:16.365 --> 00:01:22.185
So if we continue knitting over the
112 stitches and we knit in the lace

00:01:22.185 --> 00:01:27.075
pattern, the yoke's going to end up
being more than 18 inches wide instead

00:01:27.075 --> 00:01:33.555
of the desired 16 this could make
the piece really wonky and not fit.

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:36.375
So what has to happen?

00:01:37.695 --> 00:01:41.445
When it's time to switch and
start knitting the lace pattern,

00:01:41.715 --> 00:01:44.145
we need to decrease some stitches.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.600
How many?

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:54.330
16 inches at six stitches per inch
would be 96 stitches, not 112 so we

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.930
need to decrease 16 stitches evenly.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.510
How would you do that?

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.980
One way would be to remove the
knitting from the bed with a garter

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.340
bar or scrap yarn and then rehang the
work over the 96 needles, doubling

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.500
up stitches evenly across the bed.

00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:17.394
Then you could continue
knitting in the lace pattern

00:02:19.435 --> 00:02:20.905
and it would be the right size.

00:02:22.125 --> 00:02:26.265
But there definitely will be
a road differences as well.

00:02:30.204 --> 00:02:34.554
Now, if you hate math as much as I do,
there's an easier way to calculate how

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:40.614
many stitches to decrease and have the
row counts and the shaping accurate too.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.340
Just build your Knit it Now pattern twice.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:49.020
Once at each gauge, the pattern will
tell you the number of stitches at

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.310
the stockinette gauge and the number
of stitches at the lace gauge.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:58.950
Plus it will give you accurate row
counts for the lace section as well,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.160
based on your row gauge in lace.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.310
So start knitting with pattern
1at the stockinette gauge and then

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:13.500
switch to pattern 2 decreasing
stitches according to the pattern and

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.340
complete the yoke using pattern 2.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.120
If you do this, you're guaranteed
that your knitting will end up the

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.640
right size and your sweater will fit.

